Yes. Under the AI Act, in order to advise and assist the Commission and the Member States to facilitate the consistent and effective application of the Regulation, the European Artificial Intelligence Board may in particular:

* "contribute to effective cooperation with the competent authorities of third countries and with international organisations" [Art. 66(i)];
* "advise the Commission in relation to international matters on AI" [Art. 66(m)].

Yes. Member States and the Commission may exchange confidential information with regulatory authorities of third countries, subject to the following conditions [Art. 78(5)]:

* The exchange must be "where necessary" [Art. 78(5)];
* It must be "in accordance with relevant provisions of international and trade agreements" [Art. 78(5)];
* It must be conducted with regulatory authorities with which they have concluded "bilateral or multilateral confidentiality arrangements guaranteeing an adequate level of confidentiality" [Art. 78(5)].

When deciding on the amount of an administrative fine, authorities must take into account all relevant circumstances of the specific situation, giving regard to particular factors depending on the entity involved:

**For operators in general**, authorities consider [Art. 99(7)]:
* The nature, gravity, and duration of the infringement and its consequences, including the purpose of the AI system, the number of affected persons, and the level of damage suffered [Art. 99(7)(a)];
* Whether administrative fines have already been applied to the same operator for the same infringement by other market surveillance authorities [Art. 99(7)(b)], or for infringements of other Union or national law resulting from the same activity or omission [Art. 99(7)(c)];
* The size, annual turnover, and market share of the operator [Art. 99(7)(d)];
* Any other aggravating or mitigating factors, including financial benefits gained or losses avoided directly or indirectly [Art. 99(7)(e)];
* The degree of cooperation with national competent authorities to remedy the infringement and mitigate adverse effects [Art. 99(7)(f)];
* The degree of responsibility, taking into account implemented technical and organisational measures [Art. 99(7)(g)];
* How the infringement became known to the authorities, particularly whether and to what extent it was notified by the operator [Art. 99(7)(h)];
* The intentional or negligent character of the infringement [Art. 99(7)(i)];
* Any action taken by the operator to mitigate harm suffered by affected persons [Art. 99(7)(j)].

**For Union institutions, bodies, offices, and agencies**, the European Data Protection Supervisor considers [Art. 100(1)]:
* The nature, gravity, duration, and consequences of the infringement, the purpose of the AI system, the number of affected persons, and the level of damage [Art. 100(1)(a)];
* The degree of responsibility, accounting for technical and organisational measures [Art. 100(1)(b)];
* Actions taken to mitigate damage suffered by affected persons [Art. 100(1)(c)];
* The degree of cooperation with the European Data Protection Supervisor, including compliance with previously ordered measures regarding the same subject matter [Art. 100(1)(d)];
* Any similar previous infringements [Art. 100(1)(e)];
* The manner in which the infringement became known, particularly whether and to what extent it was notified [Art. 100(1)(f)];
* The annual budget of the institution, body, office, or agency [Art. 100(1)(g)].

**For providers of general-purpose AI models**, the Commission considers [Art. 101(1)]:
* The nature, gravity, and duration of the infringement, taking due account of proportionality and appropriateness [Art. 101(1)];
* Commitments made in accordance with Article 93(3) or in relevant codes of practice in accordance with Article 56 [Art. 101(1)].

gold_text:
[Art. 99(7)] 7. When deciding whether to impose an administrative fine and when deciding on the amount of the administrative fine in each individual case, all relevant circumstances of the specific situation shall be taken into account and, as appropriate, regard shall be given to the following:
(a) the nature, gravity and duration of the infringement and of its consequences, taking into account the purpose of the AI system, as well as, where appropriate, the number of affected persons and the level of damage suffered by them;
(b) whether administrative fines have already been applied by other market surveillance authorities to the same operator for the same infringement;
(c) whether administrative fines have already been applied by other authorities to the same operator for infringements of other Union or national law, when such infringements result from the same activity or omission constituting a relevant infringement of this Regulation;
(d) the size, the annual turnover and market share of the operator committing the infringement;
(e) any other aggravating or mitigating factor applicable to the circumstances of the case, such as financial benefits gained, or losses avoided, directly or indirectly, from the infringement;
(f) the degree of cooperation with the national competent authorities, in order to remedy the infringement and mitigate the possible adverse effects of the infringement;
(g) the degree of responsibility of the operator taking into account the technical and organisational measures implemented by it;
(h) the manner in which the infringement became known to the national competent authorities, in particular whether, and if so to what extent, the operator notified the infringement;
(i) the intentional or negligent character of the infringement;
(j) any action taken by the operator to mitigate the harm suffered by the affected persons.

answer:
Under Annex IX, the following information must be provided and thereafter kept up to date when registering testing in real-world conditions for a high-risk AI system [Annex IX]:

* A Union-wide unique single identification number of the testing in real-world conditions [Annex IX];
* The name and contact details of the provider or prospective provider and of the deployers involved in the testing in real-world conditions [Annex IX];
* A brief description of the AI system, its intended purpose, and other information necessary for the identification of the system [Annex IX];
* A summary of the main characteristics of the plan for testing in real-world conditions [Annex IX];
* Information on the suspension or termination of the testing in real-world conditions [Annex IX].

This is not legal advice.

gold_text:
[Annex IX] The following information shall be provided and thereafter kept up to date with regard to testing in real world conditions to be registered in accordance with Article 60:
1. A Union-wide unique single identification number of the testing in real world conditions;
2. The name and contact details of the provider or prospective provider and of the deployers involved in the testing in real world conditions;
3. A brief description of the AI system, its intended purpose, and other information necessary for the identification of the system;
4. A summary of the main characteristics of the plan for testing in real world conditions;
5. Information on the suspension or termination of the testing in real world conditions.

answer:
Under Annex VIII, Section B, points 1 to 6 of the AI Act, providers registering AI systems in accordance with Article 49(2) must provide—and thereafter keep up to date—the following details [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6]:

1. The name, address and contact details of the provider [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
2. Where the submission of information is carried out by another person on behalf of the provider, the name, address and contact details of that person [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
3. The name, address and contact details of the authorised representative, where applicable [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
4. The AI system trade name and any additional unambiguous reference allowing the identification and traceability of the AI system [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
5. A description of the intended purpose of the AI system [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
6. The condition or conditions under Article 6(3) based on which the AI system is considered to be not-high-risk [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6].

This is not legal advice.

