answer:
Under the EU AI Act, the factors considered when deciding whether to impose an administrative fine and determining its amount depend on the entity involved:

### For operators generally
All relevant circumstances of the specific situation are considered, with regard given to [Art. 99(7)]:
* **Nature, gravity, duration, and consequences:** the nature, gravity, and duration of the infringement and its consequences, considering the purpose of the AI system, and where appropriate, the number of affected persons and the level of damage suffered [Art. 99(7)(a)];
* **Prior fines:** whether administrative fines have already been applied by other market surveillance authorities for the same infringement [Art. 99(7)(b)], or by other authorities for infringements of other Union or national law resulting from the same activity or omission [Art. 99(7)(c)];
* **Operator profile:** the size, annual turnover, and market share of the operator [Art. 99(7)(d)];
* **Aggravating or mitigating factors:** any other factor, such as financial benefits gained or losses avoided directly or indirectly [Art. 99(7)(e)];
* **Cooperation:** the degree of cooperation with national competent authorities to remedy the infringement and mitigate adverse effects [Art. 99(7)(f)];
* **Responsibility and measures:** the operator's degree of responsibility, taking into account technical and organisational measures implemented [Art. 99(7)(g)];
* **Notification:** how the infringement became known to national competent authorities, particularly whether and to what extent the operator notified it [Art. 99(7)(h)];
* **Intent/negligence:** the intentional or negligent character of the infringement [Art. 99(7)(i)];
* **Mitigation of harm:** any action taken by the operator to mitigate harm suffered by affected persons [Art. 99(7)(j)].

### For Union institutions, bodies, offices, and agencies
The European Data Protection Supervisor considers all relevant circumstances and gives due regard to [Art. 100(1)]:
* The nature, gravity, and duration of the infringement and its consequences, the AI system's purpose, the number of affected persons, and the level of damage suffered [Art. 100(1)(a)];
* The degree of responsibility, taking into account implemented technical and organisational measures [Art. 100(1)(b)];
* Actions taken to mitigate damage suffered by affected persons [Art. 100(1)(c)];
* The degree of cooperation with the EDPS to remedy the infringement and mitigate adverse effects, including compliance with previously ordered measures on the same subject matter [Art. 100(1)(d)];
* Any similar previous infringements [Art. 100(1)(e)];
* The manner in which the infringement became known, especially whether and to what extent it was notified [Art. 100(1)(f)];
* The annual budget of the institution, body, office, or agency [Art. 100(1)(g)].

### For providers of general-purpose AI models
When the Commission imposes fines, it considers whether the infringement was committed "intentionally or negligently" [Art. 101(1)]. In fixing the amount, regard is had to:
* "the nature, gravity and duration of the infringement, taking due account of the principles of proportionality and appropriateness" [Art. 101(1)];
* "commitments made in accordance with Article 93(3) or made in relevant codes of practice in accordance with Article 56" [Art. 101(1)].

gold_text:
[Art. 100(1)] 1. The European Data Protection Supervisor may impose administrative fines on Union institutions, bodies, offices and agencies falling within the scope of this Regulation. When deciding whether to impose an administrative fine and when deciding on the amount of the administrative fine in each individual case, all relevant circumstances of the specific situation shall be taken into account and due regard shall be given to the following:
(a) the nature, gravity and duration of the infringement and of its consequences, taking into account the purpose of the AI system concerned, as well as, where appropriate, the number of affected persons and the level of damage suffered by them;
(b) the degree of responsibility of the Union institution, body, office or agency, taking into account technical and organisational measures implemented by them;
(c) any action taken by the Union institution, body, office or agency to mitigate the damage suffered by affected persons;
(d) the degree of cooperation with the European Data Protection Supervisor in order to remedy the infringement and mitigate the possible adverse effects of the infringement, including compliance with any of the measures previously ordered by the European Data Protection Supervisor against the Union institution, body, office or agency concerned with regard to the same subject matter;
(e) any similar previous infringements by the Union institution, body, office or agency;
(f) the manner in which the infringement became known to the European Data Protection Supervisor, in particular whether, and if so to what extent, the Union institution, body, office or agency notified the infringement;
(g) the annual budget of the Union institution, body, office or agency.

[Art. 99(7)] 7. When deciding whether to impose an administrative fine and when deciding on the amount of the administrative fine in each individual case, all relevant circumstances of the specific situation shall be taken into account and, as appropriate, regard shall be given to the following:
(a) the nature, gravity and duration of the infringement and of its consequences, taking into account the purpose of the AI system, as well as, where appropriate, the number of affected persons and the level of damage suffered by them;
(b) whether administrative fines have already been applied by other market surveillance authorities to the same operator for the same infringement;
(c) whether administrative fines have already been applied by other authorities to the same operator for infringements of other Union or national law, when such infringements result from the same activity or omission constituting a relevant infringement of this Regulation;
(d) the size, the annual turnover and market share of the operator committing the infringement;
(e) any other aggravating or mitigating factor applicable to the circumstances of the case, such as financial benefits gained, or losses avoided, directly or indirectly, from the infringement;
(f) the degree of cooperation with the national competent authorities, in order to remedy the infringement and mitigate the possible adverse effects of the infringement;
(g) the degree of responsibility of the operator taking into account the technical and organisational measures implemented by it;
(h) the manner in which the infringement became known to the national competent authorities, in particular whether, and if so to what extent, the operator notified the infringement;
(i) the intentional or negligent character of the infringement;
(j) any action taken by the operator to mitigate the harm suffered by the affected persons.

